NAME
       systemd.service - the [Service] section of service unit files

SYNOPSIS
       NAME.service

DESCRIPTION
       A service unit starts and watches a program: a long-running server
       (a daemon such as sshd) or a job that runs once and ends. Besides the
       [Unit] and [Install] sections (see systemd.unit(5)), it has a
       [Service] section, described here.

       Programs in Exec lines need their full path. They are not run by a
       shell: pipes, redirections and wildcards do not work unless you
       write ExecStart=/bin/bash -c '...'.

THE [Service] SECTION
       Type=simple|exec|forking|oneshot|notify
              How systemd knows the service has started.
              simple (the default): as soon as the program is started; the
              program stays in the foreground.
              exec: once the program has really been run.
              forking: the program starts a background copy and exits (old
              style daemons; use PIDFile=).
              oneshot: the program does a job and exits; the unit counts as
              started when it has finished. Good for scripts and timers.
              notify: the program tells systemd itself when it is ready.

       ExecStart=COMMAND
              The command to run. oneshot services may have several.

       ExecStartPre=, ExecStartPost=, ExecStop=, ExecReload=
              Commands run before or after starting, to stop, or to reload.

       Restart=no|on-failure|always|on-abnormal
              Whether to start the program again when it ends. on-failure
              restarts after a crash or a non-zero exit.

       RestartSec=TIME
              How long to wait before restarting (100ms by default).

       User=NAME, Group=NAME
              Run as this user and group instead of root.

       WorkingDirectory=DIR
              The current directory for the program.

       Environment=NAME=VALUE, EnvironmentFile=FILE
              Set environment variables, or read them from a file.

       RemainAfterExit=yes
              With oneshot: count as active after the program ended.

       TimeoutStartSec=TIME, TimeoutStopSec=TIME
              How long starting or stopping may take.

       Nice=N
              The nice value of the program (-20 to 19).

       PIDFile=PATH
              With forking: where the daemon writes its process ID.

EXAMPLES
       A service that runs a script at boot and then counts as active:

           [Unit]
           Description=Write a boot note

           [Service]
           Type=oneshot
           ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/bootnote.sh
           RemainAfterExit=yes

           [Install]
           WantedBy=multi-user.target

       A small server that should come back when it crashes, running as a
       normal user:

           [Service]
           ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 -m http.server 8000
           User=student
           Restart=on-failure

       After saving it as /etc/systemd/system/NAME.service:

           systemctl daemon-reload
           systemctl enable --now NAME.service
           systemctl status NAME.service

SEE ALSO
       systemd.unit(5), systemd.timer(5), systemctl(1), journalctl(1)
